\begin_layout Subsubsection
Multi-agent Hierarchy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this mult-agent setup,
 all participants are investors with some knowledge - but they receive information based on a long-term static order.
 There is a hierarchy in which events are received.
 Another way to look at it is that investor identity is determined solely by the speed of accurate information.
 A lot of individuals receive noisy information - but very few receive accurate information.
 All Information is accurate close to being present and definitely into past - but accuracy is low for everyone before event is past.
 A static hierarchy means that at any given point in time,
 the topmost in the hierarhcy know information with more accuracy but the lower in the order know it with less accuracy.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
one player against all others in a game of two uncertainties
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In a different but related formulation,
 the investors influence each and are more familiar with their own actions.
 This is more similar to how a war situation results in one group understanding the situation a bit better than the other.
 The game is not so clear.
 With stocks,
 one always assumes that uncertainty is intrinsic due to market - a property that is not as plausible in a game consisting of two players - a game that is more similar to risk-sharing among groups of two different sets of skills.
 Technology,
 healthcare ,
 energy are sectors with different sets of expertise that influence each other and one may get more bang for the buck by investing in each other.
 In an Schumpeterian economy,
 N players invest in assets with uncertain returns.
 Because they know more about their investments and may benefit from overstating their profit expectations,
 it is always likely that other groups hold different views - so that one group knows better about the event than others.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
